-- title:
--   Automorphism pull-back preserves regularity, simple poles and residues
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a perfect field and $F$ a field extension of $K$ admitting an element $x$ transcendental over $K$ such that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$. Let $\sigma$ be a $K$-algebra automorphism of $F$, let $v$ be a place of $F$ over $K$ — a valuation subring $\mathcal O_v \subseteq F$ containing the image of $K$, different from $F$ itself and a principal ideal ring — and let $\omega$ be a Kähler differential in $\Omega_{F/K}$. Write $w$ for the place obtained by transporting $v$ by the semilinear automorphism attached to $\sigma$ (the pair $(\sigma,\mathrm{id}_K)$ acting on places), and $\sigma^*\omega$ for the image of $\omega$ under the $K$-linear map $\Omega_{F/K} \to \Omega_{F/K}$ induced by $\sigma$. Each of $v$ and $w$ carries its chosen uniformiser $\pi$ (a chosen irreducible element of its valuation ring) and the associated differential $d\pi$. The assertion is a conjunction of three implications. First: if $\omega = f\,d\pi_v$ for some $f \in \mathcal O_v$, then $\sigma^*\omega = g\,d\pi_w$ for some $g \in \mathcal O_w$. Second: if $\omega = f\,d\pi_v$ with $\pi_v f \in \mathcal O_v$, then $\sigma^*\omega = g\,d\pi_w$ with $\pi_w g \in \mathcal O_w$. Third: for every $r \in K$, if $\omega = f\,d\pi_v$ with $\pi_v f \in \mathcal O_v$ and the residue of $\pi_v f$ in the residue field of $v$ equal to the image of $r$, then $\sigma^*\omega = g\,d\pi_w$ with $\pi_w g \in \mathcal O_w$ and residue the image of the same $r$.
--
--   This is the invariance of regularity, of having at worst a simple pole, and of the residue at a simple pole under a $K$-automorphism of a function field, stated in the normalisation by chosen uniformisers used throughout the treatment of polar differentials. It is used when diamond operators and Atkin–Lehner type automorphisms are applied to differentials on modular curves, in the verification that a differential built from a cusp form lies in the relevant space of polar differentials and has matching residues.
-- source:
